---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import GuidePage from '../components/GuidePage.astro';
import VideoSection from '../components/VideoSection.astro';
import Calculators from '../components/Calculators.astro';
import Infographic from '../components/Infographic.astro';
import { t } from '../i18n/ui';
import type { Lang } from '../i18n/utils';

const lang: Lang = 'en';
---
<Base lang={lang} path="/heroes/" title="Hero Guide: Squads, F2P Path, Skills" description="The canonical F2P squad and its full timeline, faction timing for Warriors, Rangers and Warlocks, skill upgrade priorities, star breakpoints, and the Awakened hero order for Era of Revival.">
  <GuidePage title="Hero Guide: Squads, F2P Path, Skills" description="The canonical F2P squad and its full timeline, faction timing for Warriors, Rangers and Warlocks, skill upgrade priorities, star breakpoints, and the Awakened hero order for Era of Revival." lang={lang}>
    <p>Heroes are the biggest might multiplier in Last Asylum: Plague — and the most expensive thing to fix if built wrong. Shard and skill-badge economics punish spreading investment, so the community has converged on one canonical free-to-play path and a strict order of operations around it.</p>
    <p>This page covers: which faction to ride at each stage of the game, the canonical F2P squad with its day-by-day timeline, skill priorities verified by damage-per-badge analysis, star breakpoints, and the Awakened hero progression order for the Era of Revival season.</p>

    <h2 id="faction-timing">Faction Timing: Warriors Early, Rangers Mid, Warlocks Late</h2>
    <p>The three factions are not equal at the same stage of the game. The community consensus on when each one peaks:</p>
    <table class="w-full mb-6 text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Faction</th><th>Peak window</th><th>Why</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Warriors</td><td>Early game</td><td>Free Arthur plus free Harper shards, and the $1 Marlena top-up at day 66. The game hands you a Warrior core for almost nothing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rangers</td><td>Mid game</td><td>Rangers counter Warriors — a Ranger formation takes 20% less damage from them. With investment, a mid-game Ranger squad beats roughly 98% of what it runs into.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warlocks</td><td>Late game</td><td>The strongest ceiling, but only with heavy investment — they need to overcome the Warrior boost to get there. Spend or be patient.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>For a new account this resolves a common dilemma: build the Warrior core you were given for free, even if you admire Ranger lineups. You can pivot to Rangers when your economy can actually fund one.</p>

    <h2 id="f2p-squad">The Canonical F2P Squad</h2>
    <p>The community-standard free-to-play build, assembled over the first months of an account:</p>
    <p><strong>Arthur, Daskal, Marlena, Harper, Claire</strong></p>
    <p>Three things make this the standard: every hero in it is free or near-free, the pieces arrive in an order that always gives your best squad something to grow into, and it stacks toward the Warrior mono-faction bonus that the late-game meta rewards.</p>

    <h3 id="f2p-timeline">The F2P Timeline</h3>
    <table class="w-full mb-6 text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>When</th><th>Action</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Day 1-2</td><td>Arthur arrives free and becomes your tank. Fill the remaining seats with the free Bella, Claire, and Celia shards.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Early game</td><td>Invest ONLY in Claire and Arthur. Everyone else stays a placeholder — including Bella and Celia.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Along the way</td><td>Harper to 8 stars is mandatory for the standard build. Feed him steadily; do not leave him parked at 6.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Day 66</td><td>The $1 Marlena top-up. Save UR shards so you can take her straight to 8 stars instantly — then keep pushing toward 10.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Day 120+ (Era of Revival)</td><td>Daskal joins as the best tank in the game. Claire becomes UR via the Era Day 8 conversion — her shards were free early, which is exactly why you saved them instead of spending them.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The two discipline points that make or break the build: Claire and Arthur only, early — and never eat your saved Claire shards before the Era conversion, because the conversion to UR is what those shards were hoarded for.</p>

    <h2 id="alternatives">Alternatives and Variants</h2>
    <p>The canonical five is not the only workable path — these are the recognized substitutes and regional variants.</p>
    <h3 id="no-marlena">No $1 Marlena: Cynthia Instead</h3>
    <p>Skipped the dollar? Cynthia from the day 8 Wishing Wheel is the substitute — an early-carry UR who will genuinely carry. The tradeoffs arrive late-game: you lose the 5% faction bonus by breaking the mono-faction stack, and you lose the Harper and epigraph synergy that the standard core is built around. Workable, but you will feel the missing links at the top end.</p>
    <h3 id="f2p-pvp-variant">F2P PvP Variant</h3>
    <p>For accounts that want to fight players rather than grow quietly: <strong>Arthur, Lucius or Bella, Claire, Marlena, Cynthia</strong>. It trades a piece of the faction bonus for Cynthia&apos;s PvP punch as a finisher.</p>
    <h3 id="russian-variant">The Russian Community Build</h3>
    <p>The Russian F2P community runs: Front — Arthur and Lucius (the pairing is nicknamed the Absolute Shield); Back — Claire, Kesso, and Celia. Sivir covers the early months, Harper is the long-term project. Same logic as the Western build, different pieces on the same board.</p>

    <h2 id="mono-faction-meta">The Mono-Faction Meta</h2>
    <p>Since Raven epigraphs shipped, running a single faction has gone from a nice bonus to the dominant strategy. The reason: UR epigraphs buff ONE faction only, so a stacked squad multiplies everything — the +20% five-hero bonus from the <a href="/beginners/">faction table</a>, plus the UR epigraph buffs, plus faction-specific synergies like Harper&apos;s.</p>
    <p>Which is also why the canonical squad is all-Warrior: the epigraph investment compounds on top of an already-stacked faction. Split-faction squads pay for epigraphs that only buff half the team.</p>
    <p>For spenders there is a practical exception: temporary heroes. Shadow and Cynthia get maxed to 8 stars with skills at 15-20 and then serve as event and PvP specialists — invested enough to matter, never so invested that they eat the main squad&apos;s budget.</p>

    <h2 id="skill-priorities">Skill Upgrade Priorities</h2>
    <p>Skills eat badges, and badges are scarce. The order below is verified by community damage-per-badge analysis — it is not a feel, it is measured.</p>
    <h3 id="marlena-skills">Marlena: Where the Damage Lives</h3>
    <p>Marlena is roughly <strong>85% of your squad&apos;s damage until Zoya arrives</strong>, and 50/50 with her afterward. She is the first place badges go.</p>
    <p>But there is one trap inside her kit: never max Marlena&apos;s auto-attack to 30 early. The verified order is:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Ultimate first — always.</li>
      <li>Active skill second.</li>
      <li>Auto-attack last, and only to a modest level until her other skills are done.</li>
    </ol>
    <h3 id="claire-skills">Claire: The Passive Is the Point</h3>
    <p>Claire&apos;s key skill is her passive — the team-wide damage amplifier. The verified plan: all skills to about 15, passive to 20, and hold there until Zoya arrives and reshuffles priorities.</p>
    <Infographic src="/infographics/marlena-skills.webp" alt="Marlena skill-by-skill breakdown — where her damage comes from and which skill to upgrade first" caption="Marlena&apos;s damage per skill — why the Ultimate gets every badge first" />
    <Infographic src="/infographics/skill-scaling.webp" alt="How hero skill scaling works in Last Asylum: Plague" caption="How skill scaling actually works — damage growth per skill level" />

    <h2 id="star-breakpoints">Star Breakpoints and Tenacity</h2>
    <p>Stars are the biggest single multiplier on hero might. The significant power jump arrives after <strong>4 stars</strong> — below that a hero is a seat-warmer, above it the same hero starts winning fights. This is why the community&apos;s first star target on every main-squad hero is getting through 4, not scattering shards to make everyone evenly mediocre.</p>
    <p>UR heroes have a second major breakpoint: at level 30 and 8 stars they unlock <strong>Tenacity</strong>. It is a large survivability jump and the reason 8 stars is the standard resting point for UR heroes — Marlena&apos;s day-66 instant 8 stars is designed around it.</p>

    <h2 id="awakened-order">Awakened Heroes: The Era Progression Order</h2>
    <p>In the Era of Revival season, Awakened hero progression follows one fixed order. For your main hero, complete each step before starting the next:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Main hero to 10 stars.</li>
      <li>Awakening.</li>
      <li>Exclusive Weapon.</li>
      <li>Lord Evolution.</li>
      <li>Lord Resistance.</li>
      <li>Era Enhancement — one main skin only.</li>
      <li>Only then: start the second hero through the same ladder.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The rule that binds all of it: <strong>never split Awakening materials</strong>. Awakening one hero fully outperforms awakening two heroes halfway — better one strong march than several half-finished ones. The Era-specific context for each step is in the <a href="/era/">Era of Revival guide</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="might-formula">The Might Formula</h2>
    <p>When you compare accounts, might is the sum of five separate stacks:</p>
    <table class="w-full mb-6 text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Component</th><th>Source</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Hero Might</td><td>Levels, stars, skills and gear of your heroes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Raven Might</td><td>Raven levels, gear and epigraphs — see the <a href="/raven/">Raven guide</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Soldier Might</td><td>Troop tiers and army size.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tech Bonus</td><td>Research Lab tree.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Building Bonus</td><td>City buildings — Warrior Statue, and others.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Use the split diagnostically: if your might is hero-heavy but soldier-light, you are one bad fight from losing an army you cannot replace. If it is soldier-heavy but hero-light, your marches punch below their weight. The strongest accounts keep the stacks in rough proportion — which is exactly what following the build priority order and the F2P squad timeline does for you.</p>
    <Infographic src="/infographics/might-tips.webp" alt="How to boost Might quickly in Last Asylum: Plague — the fastest might gains per investment" caption="Quick might boosts — where each point of investment pays the most" />

        <Calculators calc="ad-phase4" lang={lang} />
    <VideoSection topic="heroes" lang={lang} />
  </GuidePage>
</Base>
